{"date": "2026-11-08", "post_id": 1508000, "title": "Major Fireball Meteor", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-8-major-fireball-meteor/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2010-july-22-the-meteor-of-1860/\">The sky glows</a> with soft pinkish colors of fading twilight in this serendipitous mountaintop vista. Taken in subfreezing temperatures, the <a href=\"https://www.esa.int/ESA_Multimedia/Images/2017/11/Autumn_fireball\">thoughtfully composed photo</a> shows snowy, rugged peaks seen from a mountain pass on 2017 November 14. Below lies the village of <a href=\"https://youtu.be/bVNV2-wma5E\">La Villa, Alta Badia</a> in <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italy\">Italy</a>'s <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2024-november-6-comet-tsuchinshan-atlas-over-the-dolomites/\">Dolomite Alps</a>. Above the nestled village lights, the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/asset/hubble/constellation-ursa-major/\">constellation Ursa Major</a> hangs over the northern horizon. But most <a href=\"https://unsplash.com/photos/white-and-gray-long-fur-cat-AGehl6k8xVo\">stunning</a> is the intense <a href=\"https://cneos.jpl.nasa.gov/fireballs/intro.html\">fireball</a> meteor. It was <a href=\"https://www.esa.int/ESA_Multimedia/Images/2017/11/Autumn_fireball\">captured</a> during the camera's exposure by chance as it flashed east to west across the northern horizon, under Ursa Major's familiar <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2013-april-21-the-big-dipper/\">Big Dipper</a> <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/what-are-asterisms/\">asterism</a>. Sightings of <a href=\"https://www.amsmeteors.org/2017/11/four-major-fireballs-within-ten-hours/\">this major fireball meteor</a> were widely reported in European skies, making it one of the most reported fireball events ever for planet Earth's <a href=\"https://www.amsmeteors.org/\">American Meteor Society</a> and the <a href=\"https://www.imo.net/\">International Meteor Organization</a>. The meteor's measured track over <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Germany\">Germany</a> is consistent with its origin near the active <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-august-12-perseids-from-perseus/\">radiant</a> of November's Northern <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-august-12-perseids-from-perseus/\">Taurid Meteor Shower</a>, a shower associated with dust from <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2003-december-23-comet-encke-returns/\">Encke's comet</a>.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> witch sky?", "credit": "<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/olliemtaylor/\">Ollie Taylor</a>", "copyright": "<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/olliemtaylor/\">Ollie Taylor</a>", "alt": "A mountainous landscape with snow and trees is seen  below a starry sky. A pink band is visible on the horizon.  Coming down from the sky is a bright meteor trail. Just above the meteor are the stars of the Big Dipper.  Please see the explanation for more detailed information.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-8-major-fireball-meteor/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/meteor2017_Taylor_1200.jpg?w=0&h=0&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 November 8 - Major Fireball Meteor</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-8-major-fireball-meteor/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: The sky glows with soft pinkish colors of fading twilight in this serendipitous mountaintop vista. Taken in subfreezing temperatures, the thoughtfully composed photo shows snowy, rugged peaks seen from a mountain pass on 2017 November 14. Below lies the village of La Villa, Alta Badia in Italy&#039;s Dolomite Alps. Above the nestled village lights, the constellation Ursa Major hangs over the northern horizon. But most stunning is the intense fireball meteor. It was captured during the camera&#039;s exposure by chance as it flashed east to west across the northern horizon, under Ursa Major&#039;s familiar Big Dipper asterism. Sightings of this major fireball meteor were widely reported in European skies, making it one of the most reported fireball events ever for planet Earth&#039;s American Meteor Society and the International Meteor Organization. The meteor&#039;s measured track over Germany is consistent with its origin near the active radiant of November&#039;s Northern Taurid Meteor Shower, a shower associated with dust from Encke&#039;s comet.Tomorrow&#039;s picture: witch sky?\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"major fireball meteor\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"Major Fireball Meteor\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-8-major-fireball-meteor/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/meteor2017_Taylor_1200.jpg?w=0&#038;h=0&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/meteor2017_Taylor_1200.jpg?w=0&#038;h=0&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"A mountainous landscape with snow and trees is seen  below a starry sky. A pink band is visible on the horizon.  Coming down from the sky is a bright meteor trail. Just above the meteor are the stars of the Big Dipper.  Please see the explanation for more detailed information.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-11-08T00:05:00-05:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-11-04T10:32:05-05:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"Major Fireball Meteor\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/meteor2017_Taylor_1200.jpg?w=0&#038;h=0&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 November 8<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/meteor2017_Taylor_1200.jpg\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/meteor2017_Taylor_1200.jpg\" alt=\"A mountainous landscape with snow and trees is seen  below a starry sky. A pink band is visible on the horizon.  Coming down from the sky is a bright meteor trail. Just above the meteor are the stars of the Big Dipper.  Please see the explanation for more detailed information.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> Major Fireball Meteor </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/olliemtaylor/\">Ollie Taylor</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2010-july-22-the-meteor-of-1860/\">The sky glows</a> with soft pinkish colors of fading twilight in this serendipitous mountaintop vista. Taken in subfreezing temperatures, the <a href=\"https://www.esa.int/ESA_Multimedia/Images/2017/11/Autumn_fireball\">thoughtfully composed photo</a> shows snowy, rugged peaks seen from a mountain pass on 2017 November 14. Below lies the village of <a href=\"https://youtu.be/bVNV2-wma5E\">La Villa, Alta Badia</a> in <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italy\">Italy</a>'s <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2024-november-6-comet-tsuchinshan-atlas-over-the-dolomites/\">Dolomite Alps</a>. Above the nestled village lights, the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/asset/hubble/constellation-ursa-major/\">constellation Ursa Major</a> hangs over the northern horizon. But most <a href=\"https://unsplash.com/photos/white-and-gray-long-fur-cat-AGehl6k8xVo\">stunning</a> is the intense <a href=\"https://cneos.jpl.nasa.gov/fireballs/intro.html\">fireball</a> meteor. It was <a href=\"https://www.esa.int/ESA_Multimedia/Images/2017/11/Autumn_fireball\">captured</a> during the camera's exposure by chance as it flashed east to west across the northern horizon, under Ursa Major's familiar <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2013-april-21-the-big-dipper/\">Big Dipper</a> <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/what-are-asterisms/\">asterism</a>. Sightings of <a href=\"https://www.amsmeteors.org/2017/11/four-major-fireballs-within-ten-hours/\">this major fireball meteor</a> were widely reported in European skies, making it one of the most reported fireball events ever for planet Earth's <a href=\"https://www.amsmeteors.org/\">American Meteor Society</a> and the <a href=\"https://www.imo.net/\">International Meteor Organization</a>. The meteor's measured track over <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Germany\">Germany</a> is consistent with its origin near the active <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-august-12-perseids-from-perseus/\">radiant</a> of November's Northern <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-august-12-perseids-from-perseus/\">Taurid Meteor Shower</a>, a shower associated with dust from <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2003-december-23-comet-encke-returns/\">Encke's comet</a>.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> witch sky?\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-7-de-gerlache-kocher-massif/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nRobert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti, Keighley Rockcliffe<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261108/html"}